course: dictionary
lesson: 4742
title: Horseback: On a horse
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Horseback means riding on a horse. You can use it in a phrase, like «on horseback», or to describe something done while riding, like «horseback riding». Look at the words around it to see how it is being used. «The knight rode into battle on horseback.» Here on horseback tells you how the knight travelled.
explain: Horseback also describes an activity. «Horseback riding» means riding a horse for fun or sport. In the US people say «horseback riding», and in the UK people say «horse riding». Both mean the same thing.
explain: Context gives you clues to the meaning. «At tournaments knights jousted, charging at each other on horseback with blunted lances.» The phrase on horseback shows how the knights charged. Try your meaning in the sentence: riding a horse fits, so you are right.
fact: Horseback means riding on a horse.
fact: «On horseback» is a phrase that tells how someone travelled or fought.
fact: «Horseback riding» is the US name for what the UK calls horse riding.
fact: In history, knights, cowboys, messengers and the Mongols travelled on horseback.
fact: The saying «trust arrives on foot and leaves on horseback» uses horseback to show something going away quickly.
exercise: «The knight rode into battle on horseback.» What does on horseback mean? | *Riding on a horse | Walking on foot through the mud | Hiding in a tower | Sailing on a boat
exercise: Lily says she loves «horseback riding», and her cousin in Ohio says it too. What does it mean? | Building a stable | Sleeping in a field | *Riding a horse for fun or sport | Cooking a meal for horses
exercise: «At tournaments knights jousted, charging at each other on horseback with blunted lances.» Find and copy the two-word phrase that tells how the knights charged. | blunted lances | in front of | at tournaments | *on horseback
test: Which phrase means travelling by riding a horse? | under a bridge | on foot | in a boat | *on horseback
test: Why is «horseback riding» in the US the same as «horse riding» in the UK? | Horseback riding is only for knights | They are opposites | *Both names mean riding a horse | One means cooking, the other means riding
exam: In «An old saying goes: trust arrives on foot and leaves on horseback», which phrase shows how trust leaves? | old saying | *on horseback | arrives on foot | trust arrives
